  Things from that reading that shape the design:
  - [1mAll data is already on the page.[0m The API returns the whole 90-day window, at most 500 events. So filtering can
    happen in the browser with no API change.
  - [1mThere are exactly nine event types.[0m I'll take the list from the data contract and won't invent any client-side.
  - [1mFailed sign-ins matter most.[0m Security says the only support tickets so far came from people hunting for a failed
    sign-in they didn't recognize. That's the main use case for any filter.
  - [1mThe no-JS rule.[0m Filters are an interactive extra, so they can need JS. But today the whole table is built by
    [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m, so with JS off the page shows no events at all. That already breaks your standing rule. Fixing it would
    mean rendering the rows in the HTML, so I need your call on scope (separate question later).
  - [1mThere is no shared token sheet.[0m [38;5;153m01-foundations.md[39m says pages use CSS custom properties from a shared token sheet,
    but there isn't one in this repo. [38;5;153mactivity.css[39m uses raw hex and px values. New filter styles need tokens to pass
    review, so where they come from is also a scope question for later.

  First question: what should people be able to filter by?

  - [1mEvent type[0m (pick one or more of the nine): best fit for "find the failed sign-in". Easy to add, easy to take out.
  - [1mDate range[0m (start and end dates plus presets like "Last 7 days"): useful across 90 days. Per [38;5;153m03-forms[39m, it needs two
    date pickers, a preset Select, and start-before-end validation. That's the most UI and review surface, and the
    "When" column has to show the time zone so the boundaries mean something.
  - [1mText search[0m (matches device, location, and details): catches things like "Firefox" or "Madrid". Cheap, but fuzzy.

  I recommend [1mevent type alone[0m for this change. It solves the problem behind the tickets, keeps the diff small, and
  leaves date range for a separate commit if you want it.
